WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.520
"Resident Evil" is returning to the big&nbsp;
screen with a new live-action movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.600
"Resident Evil: Welcome to Racoon City,"&nbsp;
set for release on November 24th, 2021.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.400
Sony released the trailer for the movie on October&nbsp;
7, surprising gamers with a little mid-week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.240
zombie mayhem. The trailer also featured more than&nbsp;
a few details that only true fans would notice.

00:00:22.879 --> 00:00:25.680
"Run!"

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.600
Blink during the trailer and you might miss a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.000
reference to the iconic opening scene&nbsp;
from "Resident Evil 2" and its remake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.840
almost seamlessly recreated&nbsp;
for "Welcome to Raccoon City."

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.000
Things start innocently enough, with a truck&nbsp;
driver calmly driving down the highway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.800
listening to a radio program. Then, after&nbsp;
hearing an ominous comment about monsters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.800
on the radio, the driver encounters&nbsp;
a zombie in the middle of the road.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.520
Things don't go so well for the driver, when he&nbsp;
gets out to investigate, as the body begins to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:59.040
rise behind him, presumably to attack. Later,&nbsp;
the driver returns, fully overtaken by whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.840
affliction is affecting the city, driving&nbsp;
his semi straight toward Leon and Claire.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.960
The original "Resident Evil&nbsp;
2" featured a similar scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.440
so it only makes sense that they would recreate&nbsp;
the sequence for a "Resident Evil" movie."

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.680
The trailer for "Welcome to Raccoon City"&nbsp;
shows a driver that looks extremely similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.320
to the one in the games, sporting the same&nbsp;
well-groomed beard and hat. In the trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.960
the driver's eyes are completely black, with&nbsp;
dark veins creeping along the edges of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.000
face. While the short sequence doesn't show&nbsp;
him chasing Claire or Leon, viewers do get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.960
to see his semi overturn, crashing right in&nbsp;
front of the Raccoon City Police Department.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.400
"Every story has a beginning."

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.360
Fans who have played the original "Resident&nbsp;
Evil" or its remake will no doubt remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.320
the bone-chilling experience of stumbling&nbsp;
across the game's very first zombie. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.400
"Resident Evil: Welcome to Raccoon City" wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.160
you to feel that same sense of terror&nbsp;
when you watch it on the big screen.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.760
The movie trailer shows a scene with a&nbsp;
zombie in a lab coat that's in the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.680
same position as the first zombie you&nbsp;
meet in the original "Resident Evil."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.840
It certainly looks more faithful to the&nbsp;
games than those Milla Jovovich movies.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.160
"People are getting sick."

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.320
One reference in the trailer is&nbsp;
just two words: "itchy" and "tasty."

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.960
This is a reference to an infamous journal that&nbsp;
players discovered back in the original "Resident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:21.520
Evil" game. The harrowing diary told the tale&nbsp;
of someone's descent into becoming a zombie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.840
with the last haunting entry&nbsp;
simply reading "Itchy. Tasty."

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.600
The two words became iconic for the series,&nbsp;
to the point where the official "Resident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.560
Evil" Twitter account actually tweeted the&nbsp;
words in August 2021 to get fans hyped for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.280
the upcoming movie. It worked out, as the&nbsp;
simple phrase earned over 20,000 likes.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.280
"We need to expose Umbrella."

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.680
The trailer for "Resident Evil: Welcome to&nbsp;
Racoon City" uses iconic locations like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.040
Spencer Mansion and Police Station&nbsp;
to demonstrate that the film will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.520
pull elements from both "Resident Evil"&nbsp;
and "Resident Evil 2" to tell its story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.640
That includes some of the&nbsp;
series' most infamous characters.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.880
At one point, a dossier appears on screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.720
and fans who look closely will&nbsp;
notice two critical names.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.120
Printed at the top of the file is Lisa Trevor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.200
whose story was featured heavily in the&nbsp;
remake of the first "Resident Evil" game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.760
A tragic figure who was experimented&nbsp;
on and used to create the G-Virus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.320
she's easily one of the scariest "Resident Evil"&nbsp;
villains. It's also worth noting that a clue at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.760
the bottom of the dossier may reveal that her&nbsp;
movie origin story will match that of the games.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.560
The signature on the file reads "W. Birkin," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.360
"Resident Evil" fans know can stand&nbsp;
for only one man: Dr. William Birkin.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.480
According to Deadline, Birkin will be played by&nbsp;
Neal McDonough, and if fans back up the trailer a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.600
couple of frames, they will see him as the evil&nbsp;
virologist who not only experimented on Lisa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.800
but also kicked off the horrific&nbsp;
events of "Resident Evil 2."

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.320
"Good God, William. What have you done?"

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.480
On top of the dossier is what looks like a&nbsp;
film canister with the label "Ashford Twins,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.160
tying the movie into the events of&nbsp;
"Resident Evil - Code: Veronica."

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.320
"You don't fool me! I am Alfred Ashford!"

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.800
The opening moments of the "Resident Evil:&nbsp;
Welcome to Racoon City" trailer waste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.920
no time in letting fans know that things are not&nbsp;
going well under the Umbrella Corporation's watch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.600
An early moment in the trailer highlights&nbsp;
members of the Umbrella Security Service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.120
or U.S.S., patrolling a locked-down Raccoon City.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:30.560
One shot stands out amongst the rest. While it&nbsp;
can be tough to be sure without more context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.040
a soldier wearing a gas mask has&nbsp;
a definite resemblance to HUNK.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.560
HUNK is the codename of an Umbrella Operative also&nbsp;
known as "Mr. Death," due to his seeming ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.000
to survive every major Umbrella-related disaster.&nbsp;
His exploits may not always be canonical to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.680
games, but he's a popular character among fans&nbsp;
— and quite a deep-cut reference for this movie.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.360
Again, it's hard to confirm that the&nbsp;
gas-masked individual is actually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:00.080
legendary U.S.S. operative, but it could&nbsp;
provide a possible set-up for future films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.280
HUNK was, after all, the character who extracted&nbsp;
samples of the G-Virus and the t-virus after an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.240
encounter with Dr. Birkin, so it's not out&nbsp;
of the question that he's in the movie too.

